The Intelligence–Attention Paradox: A Mathematical Theory of Cognitive Allocation Under Algorithmic Selection and AI Abundance

Ouadi Maakoul & SI TEAM

Theoretical Working Paper — v0.4 (Mathematical Foundations, Proofs, and Algorithms)

October 2026

---

Abstract

We develop a formal mathematical theory of cognitive allocation under algorithmic selection and AI-generated content abundance. The central object is a non-convex attention allocation problem in which a user with finite attentional budget B allocates attention A_i across content objects with sigmoidal cognitive production functions D_i(A_i). We prove that optimal allocations exhibit a minimal activation threshold A_i^\dagger: no optimal allocation ever falls in the sub-threshold region (0, A_i^\dagger). We extend the model with item-specific friction \tau_i, proving that friction inflates activation thresholds and generates a critical exclusion threshold \tau_i^{\text{crit}} beyond which content is driven out of the active set regardless of intrinsic depth. We formalize platform-induced distortions as a two-stage game between a platform maximizing engagement and a user maximizing cognitive depth, deriving a cognitive deadweight loss \Delta D_{\text{loss}} and proving that platform filtering fragments the active set, degrades average efficiency, and suppresses the shadow price of attention. We propose a structural equation model for the latent attentional expenditure estimator \widehat{A}_h and specify three falsification criteria. Algorithms for allocation, estimation, and distortion computation are provided.

Keywords: cognitive production function, non-convex allocation, attention economics, algorithmic recommendation, rational inattention, information foraging, generative AI

