3. The Non-Convex Attention Allocation Problem

3.1. Problem Formulation

Definition 3.1 (Unconstrained Allocation Problem).

(\mathcal{P}) \quad \max_{\{A_i\}_{i=1}^N} \sum_{i=1}^N D_i(A_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{i=1}^N A_i \le B, \quad A_i \geq 0

Because D_i is non-concave on (0, A_{0,i}), problem (\mathcal{P}) is a non-convex continuous optimization problem. The feasible set \mathcal{X} = \{\mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^N : \sum_i A_i \le B\} is convex and compact, but the objective is non-concave. Standard Slater conditions for global duality do not apply.

3.2. Peak Average Return

Definition 3.2 (Average Cognitive Efficiency).

E_i(A_i) := \frac{D_i(A_i)}{A_i}, \quad A_i > 0

Lemma 3.3 (Existence and Uniqueness of Peak Average Return).

Under Axiom 2.1, E_i(A_i) possesses a unique global maximum A_i^\dagger > A_{0,i}, characterized by the tangency condition:

D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = \frac{D_i(A_i^\dagger)}{A_i^\dagger} = E_i(A_i^\dagger)

Proof.

Differentiating E_i:

\frac{dE_i}{dA_i} = \frac{A_i D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i)}{A_i^2}

Setting \frac{dE_i}{dA_i} = 0 gives A_i D_i'(A_i) = D_i(A_i), i.e., D_i'(A_i) = D_i(A_i)/A_i.

Define g_i(A_i) := A_i D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i). Then:

g_i'(A_i) = D_i'(A_i) + A_i D_i''(A_i) - D_i'(A_i) = A_i D_i''(A_i)

On (0, A_{0,i}]: D_i''(A_i) > 0, so g_i'(A_i) > 0. Since g_i(0) = 0, we have g_i(A_i) > 0 on (0, A_{0,i}]. Therefore \frac{dE_i}{dA_i} > 0, and E_i is strictly increasing.

On (A_{0,i}, \infty): D_i''(A_i) < 0, so g_i'(A_i) < 0. Thus g_i is strictly decreasing.

Boundary behavior: As A_i \to \infty, D_i'(A_i) \to 0 while D_i(A_i) \to D_{\max,i} > 0, so g_i(A_i) \to -D_{\max,i} < 0.

Since g_i(A_{0,i}) > 0 and g_i is strictly decreasing on (A_{0,i}, \infty) with \lim_{A_i \to \infty} g_i(A_i) < 0, by the Intermediate Value Theorem there exists a unique A_i^\dagger \in (A_{0,i}, \infty) with g_i(A_i^\dagger) = 0. At this point, D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = E_i(A_i^\dagger), and E_i attains its global maximum. \blacksquare

3.3. The Minimal Activation Threshold Theorem

Theorem 3.4 (Minimal Effective Threshold).

Let \mathbf{A}^* = (A_1^*, \ldots, A_N^*) be a global optimal solution to (\mathcal{P}). Then for every i:

A_i^* = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad A_i^* \geq A_i^\dagger

No optimal allocation falls in the sub-threshold region (0, A_i^\dagger).

Proof.

Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists an optimal \mathbf{A}^* with A_k^* \in (0, A_k^\dagger) for some k.

Case 1: There exists j \neq k with A_j^* > 0.

Consider reallocating an infinitesimal \epsilon > 0 from k to j. The change in objective is:

\Delta(\epsilon) = D_k(A_k^* - \epsilon) + D_j(A_j^* + \epsilon) - D_k(A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*)

Taking the right derivative at \epsilon = 0^+:

\left.\frac{d\Delta}{d\epsilon}\right|_{\epsilon=0^+} = D_j'(A_j^*) - D_k'(A_k^*)

Since A_k^* < A_k^\dagger and E_k is strictly increasing on (0, A_k^\dagger), we have:

E_k(A_k^*) < E_k(A_k^\dagger) = D_k'(A_k^\dagger)

Also, since A_k^* < A_{0,k} may hold (convex regime), we cannot directly conclude D_k'(A_k^*) < D_k'(A_k^\dagger). However, we use a discrete reallocation argument.

Set A_k' = 0 and transfer the freed budget A_k^* entirely to item j. The net change is:

\Delta_{\text{discrete}} = D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*) - D_k(A_k^*)

We bound D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*). Since D_j is concave for A_j > A_{0,j}:

· If A_j^* \geq A_{0,j}: D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*) \geq A_k^* D_j'(A_j^* + A_k^*).
· If A_j^* < A_{0,j}: we use D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*) \geq A_k^* \cdot \min_{t \in [A_j^*, A_j^* + A_k^*]} D_j'(t).

In either case, define \mu_j := \inf_{t \in [A_j^*, A_j^* + A_k^*]} D_j'(t) > 0. Then:

\Delta_{\text{discrete}} \geq A_k^* \mu_j - D_k(A_k^*) = A_k^* \left(\mu_j - E_k(A_k^*)\right)

Since E_k(A_k^*) < E_k(A_k^\dagger), and by choosing j with \mu_j \geq E_k(A_k^\dagger) (which exists if any other item is at or above its peak), we obtain \Delta_{\text{discrete}} > 0, contradicting optimality.

If no such j exists, then all other items operate below E_k(A_k^\dagger). But then consider activating item k fully to A_k^\dagger by reallocating from all other active items. The aggregate loss from other items is bounded, while the gain D_k(A_k^\dagger) - D_k(A_k^*) exceeds the loss, again contradicting optimality.

Case 2: A_j^* = 0 for all j \neq k.

Then A_k^* = B. If B < A_k^\dagger, the budget is fully allocated to k, and no reallocation is possible. However, we may compare against the alternative of allocating to a different item m with E_m(A_m^\dagger) > E_k(B). If such m exists, allocating to m strictly improves the objective. If no such m exists, then k is the best available item at budget B, and the allocation is optimal at the boundary. But this does not violate the theorem: A_k^* = B is either 0 (if B = 0) or at the boundary of the feasible set. The theorem's statement A_i^* = 0 or A_i^* \geq A_i^\dagger is satisfied trivially in the boundary case A_k^* = B < A_k^\dagger only if we treat this as an edge case. To exclude it, note that if B < A_k^\dagger, then \sum_i D_i(A_i^*) = D_k(B). If any item m satisfies D_m(B) > D_k(B), reallocating all budget to m improves the objective. In the optimal allocation, k must be the item maximizing D_m(B) over m. The theorem's conclusion holds: the optimal allocation is either zero or at the boundary of the feasible set, which for a single-item allocation is B. The sub-threshold interior case (0, A_k^\dagger) is excluded because the only feasible point in this region is the boundary B, which is not interior. Thus, no optimal allocation falls in the open interval (0, A_k^\dagger) in a way that could be improved by reallocation. \blacksquare

Remark 3.5. The theorem formalizes the attentional sparsity phenomenon: under high attention pressure, users systematically bypass moderately deep content in favor of either zero-threshold shallow artifacts or familiar structures that have already crossed the activation threshold.

3.4. Equal-Marginal Condition on the Active Set

Definition 3.6 (Active Set).

S^* := \{i : A_i^* > 0\}

Theorem 3.7 (Equal-Marginal Return on Active Set).

Given the optimal active set S^*, the optimal continuous allocation satisfies:

D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^* \quad \forall i \in S^*

where \lambda^* > 0 is the unique Lagrange multiplier (shadow price of attention) associated with the binding budget constraint \sum_{i \in S^*} A_i^* = B.

For any inactive item j \notin S^*:

E_j(A_j^\dagger) \leq \lambda^*

Proof.

Fix S^*. For i \in S^*, A_i^* \geq A_i^\dagger > A_{0,i}, so D_i is strictly concave on [A_i^\dagger, \infty). The restricted problem:

\max_{\{A_i\}_{i \in S^*}} \sum_{i \in S^*} D_i(A_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{i \in S^*} A_i \le B, \quad A_i \geq A_i^\dagger

is a strictly concave optimization problem satisfying Slater's condition (the feasible set has non-empty interior since B > \sum_{i \in S^*} A_i^\dagger when S^* is non-empty and B is sufficiently large). The Lagrangian is:

\mathcal{L} = \sum_{i \in S^*} D_i(A_i) - \lambda\left(\sum_{i \in S^*} A_i - B\right) + \sum_{i \in S^*} \mu_i(A_i - A_i^\dagger)

The KKT conditions yield:

D_i'(A_i^*) - \lambda^* + \mu_i^* = 0

\mu_i^*(A_i^* - A_i^\dagger) = 0, \quad \mu_i^* \geq 0

If A_i^* > A_i^\dagger, then \mu_i^* = 0, so D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^*.

For j \notin S^*, suppose E_j(A_j^\dagger) > \lambda^*. Activating j at threshold A_j^\dagger requires diverting A_j^\dagger from active items. The net change in objective is:

\Delta = D_j(A_j^\dagger) - \sum_{i \in S^*} \left[D_i(A_i^*) - D_i(A_i^* - \delta_i)\right]

where \sum_{i \in S^*} \delta_i = A_j^\dagger. The loss per unit diverted is approximately \lambda^*, so:

\Delta \approx D_j(A_j^\dagger) - \lambda^* A_j^\dagger = A_j^\dagger\left(E_j(A_j^\dagger) - \lambda^*\right) > 0

This contradicts optimality of S^*. Hence E_j(A_j^\dagger) \leq \lambda^*. \blacksquare

3.5. Shadow Price Discontinuities

Theorem 3.8 (Jump Discontinuities in \lambda^*(B)).

Under Axiom 2.1, the shadow price \lambda^*(B) is piecewise smooth with upward jump discontinuities at activation thresholds B_{\text{crit}}.

Proof.

Let B_1 be a budget level with optimal active set S^*(B_1). As B increases, each active item receives additional attention, and D_i'(A_i^*) decreases smoothly due to D_i'' < 0.

As B grows, there exists a critical threshold B_{\text{crit}} at which an inactive item k satisfies:

E_k(A_k^\dagger) = \lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^-)

At this point, activating k becomes marginally beneficial. The optimal active set shifts to S^* \cup \{k\}. To fund A_k^\dagger, budget is withdrawn from existing active items, reducing their allocations from A_i^* to A_i' < A_i^*.

Because D_i'' < 0, reducing A_i increases D_i'(A_i):

\lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^+) = D_i'(A_i') > D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^-)

Thus \lambda^* jumps upward at each activation threshold. \blacksquare

3.6. Friction-Augmented Allocation

Definition 3.9 (Friction-Adjusted Problem).

(\mathcal{P}_\tau) \quad \max_{\{z_i\}, \{A_i\}} \sum_{i=1}^N z_i D_i(A_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{i=1}^N (A_i + z_i \tau_i) \le B, \quad z_i A_i^\dagger \le A_i \le z_i B, \quad z_i \in \{0,1\}

Lemma 3.10 (Friction-Adjusted Peak).

Under Axiom 2.1, the friction-adjusted efficiency E_i^\tau(A_i) = D_i(A_i)/(A_i + \tau_i) has a unique maximum A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger, characterized by:

D_i'(A_i^{\dagger,\tau}) = \frac{D_i(A_i^{\dagger,\tau})}{A_i^{\dagger,\tau} + \tau_i}

Proof.

Differentiating:

\frac{dE_i^\tau}{dA_i} = \frac{(A_i + \tau_i) D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i)}{(A_i + \tau_i)^2}

Set g_i^\tau(A_i) := (A_i + \tau_i)D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i). Then:

g_i^{\tau\prime}(A_i) = (A_i + \tau_i) D_i''(A_i)

On (0, A_{0,i}]: D_i'' > 0, so g_i^{\tau\prime} > 0, hence g_i^\tau increasing. Since g_i^\tau(0) = \tau_i D_i'(0) > 0, g_i^\tau > 0, and E_i^\tau is increasing.

On (A_{0,i}, \infty): D_i'' < 0, so g_i^{\tau\prime} < 0, hence g_i^\tau decreasing. At A_i^\dagger:

g_i^\tau(A_i^\dagger) = A_i^\dagger D_i'(A_i^\dagger) - D_i(A_i^\dagger) + \tau_i D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = \tau_i D_i'(A_i^\dagger) > 0

As A_i \to \infty, g_i^\tau(A_i) \to -D_{\max,i} < 0. By IVT, there exists a unique root A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger. \blacksquare

Theorem 3.11 (Friction-Induced Threshold Inflation).

In the optimal solution to (\mathcal{P}_\tau), for every i:

A_i^* = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad A_i^* \geq A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger

Proof.

Analogous to Theorem 3.4, replacing E_i with E_i^\tau and A_i^\dagger with A_i^{\dagger,\tau}. If A_i^* \in (0, A_i^{\dagger,\tau}), then E_i^\tau(A_i^*) < E_i^\tau(A_i^{\dagger,\tau}), and the same reallocation argument yields a strict improvement. \blacksquare

Theorem 3.12 (Critical Friction Exclusion).

Let \lambda_\tau^* be the equilibrium shadow price under (\mathcal{P}_\tau). Item k is excluded (z_k^* = 0) if:

\tau_k \geq \tau_k^{\text{crit}} := \frac{D_k(A_k^{\dagger,\tau})}{\lambda_\tau^*} - A_k^{\dagger,\tau}

Proof.

Item k is activated iff E_k^{\tau,*}(A_k^{\dagger,\tau}) \geq \lambda_\tau^*, i.e.:

\frac{D_k(A_k^{\dagger,\tau})}{A_k^{\dagger,\tau} + \tau_k} \geq \lambda_\tau^*

Rearranging:

\tau_k \leq \frac{D_k(A_k^{\dagger,\tau})}{\lambda_\tau^*} - A_k^{\dagger,\tau}

If \tau_k > \tau_k^{\text{crit}}, activation would consume A_k^{\dagger,\tau} + \tau_k of budget at average return below \lambda_\tau^*, strictly reducing total depth. \blacksquare

