4. Platform-Induced Distortions

4.1. The Two-Stage Game

Definition 4.1 (Platform Selection Operator).

\mathcal{C}_p = \arg\max_{S \subset \mathcal{C}, |S| \leq K} \sum_{i \in S} \phi_p(V_i, T_i)

where \phi_p is strictly increasing in visibility V_i and exposure T_i, and conditionally independent of D_i(A_i) by Axiom 2.3.

Definition 4.2 (Restricted Allocation Problem).

(\mathcal{P}_p) \quad \max_{\{A_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{C}_p}} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{C}_p} D_i(A_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{i \in \mathcal{C}_p} A_i \le B, \quad A_i \geq 0

4.2. Cognitive Deadweight Loss

Theorem 4.3 (Cognitive Deadweight Loss).

Let D^*(\mathcal{C}) denote the optimal value of (\mathcal{P}) and D^*(\mathcal{C}_p) the optimal value of (\mathcal{P}_p). Then:

(i) D^*(\mathcal{C}_p) = D^*(\mathcal{C}) iff S^* \subseteq \mathcal{C}_p.

(ii) If S^* \setminus \mathcal{C}_p \neq \emptyset, then:

\Delta D_{\text{loss}} := D^*(\mathcal{C}) - D^*(\mathcal{C}_p) > 0

Proof.

(i) Sufficiency. If S^* \subseteq \mathcal{C}_p, then the unconstrained optimum \mathbf{A}^* has A_j^* = 0 for all j \notin S^* \subseteq \mathcal{C}_p. Hence \mathbf{A}^* is feasible for (\mathcal{P}_p). Since \mathbf{A}^* maximizes over the larger feasible set \mathcal{X}, it also maximizes over \mathcal{X}_p \subset \mathcal{X}.

(ii) Necessity. If k \in S^* \setminus \mathcal{C}_p, then A_k^* \geq A_k^\dagger > 0 and D_k'(A_k^*) = \lambda^*. In (\mathcal{P}_p), the constraint A_k = 0 is imposed. Since D is strictly concave on the active subspace [A_i^\dagger, \infty)^{|S^*|}, the unconstrained optimum \mathbf{A}^* is the unique maximizer. Restricting to \mathcal{X}_p \subsetneq \mathcal{X} forces a strictly lower maximum. \blacksquare

4.3. Active Set Fragmentation and Shadow Price Suppression

Theorem 4.4 (Threshold Crowding-Out).

Suppose the platform filter replaces high-depth item k \in S^* with shallow items M_{\text{shallow}} \subset \mathcal{C}_p \setminus S^* satisfying A_m^\dagger < A_k^\dagger and E_m(A_m^\dagger) < E_k(A_k^\dagger) for all m \in M_{\text{shallow}}. Then:

(i) Fragmentation. |S_p^*| > |S^*|.

(ii) Efficiency Degradation. \frac{D^*(\mathcal{C}_p)}{B} < \frac{D^*(\mathcal{C})}{B}.

(iii) Shadow Price Suppression. \lambda_p^* < \lambda^*.

Proof.

(i) The freed budget A_k^* \geq A_k^\dagger can activate multiple shallow items simultaneously since A_k^* \geq \sum_{m \in M_{\text{active}}} A_m^\dagger for |M_{\text{active}}| > 1. Each activated m has E_m(A_m^\dagger) \geq \lambda_p^*, so |S_p^*| = |S^* \cap \mathcal{C}_p| + |M_{\text{active}}| > |S^*|.

(ii) By Theorem 4.3, D^*(\mathcal{C}_p) < D^*(\mathcal{C}). Dividing by B > 0: D^*(\mathcal{C}_p)/B < D^*(\mathcal{C})/B.

(iii) To absorb the excess budget, shallow items move further into their concave tails where D_m'' \ll 0. Thus:

\lambda_p^* = D_m'(A_{m,p}^*) < D_m'(A_m^\dagger) = E_m(A_m^\dagger) < E_k(A_k^\dagger) = \lambda^*

\blacksquare

