5. The \Phi Indicator and Its Relation to Allocation

5.1. Definition and Equivalence Condition

Definition 5.1 (Cognitive Efficiency Indicator).

\Phi(C, u) := \frac{D(C)}{A_h(C, u)}

Proposition 5.2 (Equivalence with Marginal Condition).

\Phi_i(A_i^*) = \lambda^* for all i \in S^* if and only if D_i is linear on [A_i^\dagger, \infty).

Proof.

\Phi_i(A_i^*) = \lambda^* means D_i(A_i^*)/A_i^* = D_i'(A_i^*). This is the tangency condition for the peak average return, which holds at A_i^\dagger, not at A_i^* > A_i^\dagger unless D_i is linear. If D_i(A_i) = \alpha A_i, then D_i'(A_i) = \alpha = D_i(A_i)/A_i for all A_i, so \Phi_i = \lambda^* identically. \blacksquare

Corollary 5.3. In general, \Phi \neq \lambda^*. The ratio \Phi measures average efficiency, while \lambda^* measures marginal value. They coincide only under linear production.

5.2. Rival Functional Forms

Form Expression Coincides with \lambda^* when
Ratio D/A_h D linear
Power-ratio D/A_h^\alpha \alpha = 1, D linear
Difference D - \lambda A_h Never (different object)
Cobb–Douglas D^\beta A_h^{-\alpha} \beta = 1, \alpha = 1
Budget-constrained \max \sum D_i s.t. \sum A_i \le B Always (fundamental)

