10. Conclusion

We have developed a complete mathematical theory of cognitive allocation under algorithmic selection and AI abundance. The theory rests on three pillars:

1. Non-convex production. Cognitive production functions are sigmoidal, generating minimal activation thresholds A_i^\dagger and discrete active sets.
2. Friction-augmented allocation. Item-specific friction \tau_i inflates thresholds to A_i^{\dagger,\tau} and generates critical exclusion thresholds \tau_i^{\text{crit}}.
3. Platform-induced distortion. Engagement-optimizing filters fragment active sets, degrade average efficiency, and suppress the shadow price of attention, generating a measurable deadweight loss \Delta D_{\text{loss}}.

The ratio \Phi = D/A_h is a descriptive indicator, not the fundamental object. The fundamental object is the non-convex allocation problem (\mathcal{P}) with solution characterized by D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^* on S^*. The Intelligence–Attention Paradox is the observation that:

\boxed{
\text{AI} \uparrow \Rightarrow C \uparrow \Rightarrow \mathcal{E} \uparrow \Rightarrow \lambda^* \text{ distorted} \Rightarrow \Delta D_{\text{loss}} > 0
}

The more intelligence humanity can generate, the more valuable scarce human attention becomes — and the more consequential its misallocation.

