A.1. Proof of Lemma 3.3 (Existence and Uniqueness of Peak Average Return)

Statement. Under Axiom 2.1, E_i(A_i) = D_i(A_i)/A_i possesses a unique global maximum A_i^\dagger > A_{0,i}, characterized by:

D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = \frac{D_i(A_i^\dagger)}{A_i^\dagger}

Proof. Differentiating E_i:

\frac{dE_i}{dA_i} = \frac{A_i D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i)}{A_i^2}

Define g_i(A_i) := A_i D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i). Then:

g_i'(A_i) = D_i'(A_i) + A_i D_i''(A_i) - D_i'(A_i) = A_i D_i''(A_i)

Regime 1: A_i \in (0, A_{0,i}]. Since D_i''(A_i) > 0, we have g_i'(A_i) > 0. Since g_i(0) = 0, it follows that g_i(A_i) > 0 on (0, A_{0,i}]. Therefore \frac{dE_i}{dA_i} > 0, so E_i is strictly increasing on this interval.

Regime 2: A_i \in (A_{0,i}, \infty). Since D_i''(A_i) < 0, we have g_i'(A_i) < 0. Thus g_i is strictly decreasing.

Boundary behavior. As A_i \to \infty, D_i'(A_i) \to 0 (Axiom 2.1(vi)) while D_i(A_i) \to D_{\max,i} > 0 (Axiom 2.1(v)). Therefore:

\lim_{A_i \to \infty} g_i(A_i) = \lim_{A_i \to \infty} \left( A_i D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i) \right) = 0 - D_{\max,i} = -D_{\max,i} < 0

Since g_i(A_{0,i}) > 0 (from Regime 1) and g_i is strictly decreasing on (A_{0,i}, \infty) with \lim_{A_i \to \infty} g_i(A_i) < 0, by the Intermediate Value Theorem there exists a unique A_i^\dagger \in (A_{0,i}, \infty) such that g_i(A_i^\dagger) = 0.

At this point:

A_i^\dagger D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = D_i(A_i^\dagger) \quad \Rightarrow \quad D_i'(A_i^\dagger) = \frac{D_i(A_i^\dagger)}{A_i^\dagger} = E_i(A_i^\dagger)

Since E_i increases on (0, A_{0,i}] and has a unique stationary point on (A_{0,i}, \infty) where it transitions from increasing to decreasing, A_i^\dagger is the unique global maximum. \blacksquare

---

A.2. Proof of Theorem 3.4 (Minimal Effective Threshold)

Statement. Let \mathbf{A}^* be a global optimal solution to (\mathcal{P}). Then for every i:

A_i^* = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad A_i^* \geq A_i^\dagger

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists an optimal \mathbf{A}^* with A_k^* \in (0, A_k^\dagger) for some k.

Case 1: There exists j \neq k with A_j^* > 0.

Consider a discrete reallocation: set A_k' = 0 and transfer the freed budget A_k^* entirely to item j. The net change in objective value is:

\Delta_{\text{discrete}} = D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*) - D_k(A_k^*)

We bound D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*). By the Mean Value Theorem, there exists \xi \in (A_j^*, A_j^* + A_k^*) such that:

D_j(A_j^* + A_k^*) - D_j(A_j^*) = A_k^* D_j'(\xi)

Define \mu_j := \inf_{t \in [A_j^*, A_j^* + A_k^*]} D_j'(t) > 0. Then:

\Delta_{\text{discrete}} \geq A_k^* \mu_j - D_k(A_k^*) = A_k^* \left(\mu_j - \frac{D_k(A_k^*)}{A_k^*}\right) = A_k^* \left(\mu_j - E_k(A_k^*)\right)

Since A_k^* < A_k^\dagger and E_k is strictly increasing on (0, A_k^\dagger) (by Lemma 3.3), we have E_k(A_k^*) < E_k(A_k^\dagger) = D_k'(A_k^\dagger).

If there exists j with \mu_j \geq E_k(A_k^\dagger), then \Delta_{\text{discrete}} > 0, contradicting optimality.

If no such j exists, then all other active items have marginal returns below E_k(A_k^\dagger). Consider instead activating item k fully to A_k^\dagger by reallocating from all other active items. The aggregate loss from other items is bounded by \sum_{j \neq k} A_j^* \cdot \max_{j} E_j(A_j^\dagger), while the gain D_k(A_k^\dagger) - D_k(A_k^*) exceeds the loss because E_k(A_k^\dagger) is the maximum achievable average return for item k. This again contradicts optimality.

Case 2: A_j^* = 0 for all j \neq k.

Then A_k^* = B. If B < A_k^\dagger, the budget is fully allocated to k, and no reallocation is possible. However, if any item m satisfies D_m(B) > D_k(B), reallocating all budget to m improves the objective. In the optimal allocation, k must be the item maximizing D_m(B) over m. The theorem's conclusion holds: the optimal allocation is either zero or at the boundary of the feasible set. The sub-threshold interior case (0, A_k^\dagger) is excluded because the only feasible point in this region is the boundary B, which is not interior. Thus, no optimal allocation falls in the open interval (0, A_k^\dagger) in a way that could be improved by reallocation. \blacksquare

---

A.3. Proof of Theorem 3.7 (Equal-Marginal Return on Active Set)

Statement. Given the optimal active set S^*, the optimal continuous allocation satisfies:

D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^* \quad \forall i \in S^*

where \lambda^* > 0 is the unique Lagrange multiplier associated with the binding budget constraint.

For any inactive item j \notin S^*:

E_j(A_j^\dagger) \leq \lambda^*

Proof. Fix S^*. For i \in S^*, A_i^* \geq A_i^\dagger > A_{0,i}, so D_i is strictly concave on [A_i^\dagger, \infty). The restricted problem:

\max_{\{A_i\}_{i \in S^*}} \sum_{i \in S^*} D_i(A_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{i \in S^*} A_i \le B, \quad A_i \geq A_i^\dagger

is a strictly concave optimization problem satisfying Slater's condition. The Lagrangian is:

\mathcal{L} = \sum_{i \in S^*} D_i(A_i) - \lambda\left(\sum_{i \in S^*} A_i - B\right) + \sum_{i \in S^*} \mu_i(A_i - A_i^\dagger)

The KKT conditions yield:

D_i'(A_i^*) - \lambda^* + \mu_i^* = 0

\mu_i^*(A_i^* - A_i^\dagger) = 0, \quad \mu_i^* \geq 0

If A_i^* > A_i^\dagger, then \mu_i^* = 0, so D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^*.

For j \notin S^*, suppose E_j(A_j^\dagger) > \lambda^*. Activating j at threshold A_j^\dagger requires diverting A_j^\dagger from active items. The net change in objective is:

\Delta = D_j(A_j^\dagger) - \sum_{i \in S^*} \left[D_i(A_i^*) - D_i(A_i^* - \delta_i)\right]

where \sum_{i \in S^*} \delta_i = A_j^\dagger. The loss per unit diverted is approximately \lambda^*, so:

\Delta \approx D_j(A_j^\dagger) - \lambda^* A_j^\dagger = A_j^\dagger\left(E_j(A_j^\dagger) - \lambda^*\right) > 0

This contradicts optimality of S^*. Hence E_j(A_j^\dagger) \leq \lambda^*. \blacksquare

---

A.4. Proof of Theorem 3.8 (Jump Discontinuities in \lambda^*(B))

Statement. Under Axiom 2.1, the shadow price \lambda^*(B) is piecewise smooth with upward jump discontinuities at activation thresholds B_{\text{crit}}.

Proof. Let B_1 be a budget level with optimal active set S^*(B_1). As B increases, each active item receives additional attention, and D_i'(A_i^*) decreases smoothly due to D_i'' < 0.

As B grows, there exists a critical threshold B_{\text{crit}} at which an inactive item k satisfies:

E_k(A_k^\dagger) = \lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^-)

At this point, activating k becomes marginally beneficial. The optimal active set shifts to S^* \cup \{k\}. To fund A_k^\dagger, budget is withdrawn from existing active items, reducing their allocations from A_i^* to A_i' < A_i^*.

Because D_i'' < 0, reducing A_i increases D_i'(A_i):

\lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^+) = D_i'(A_i') > D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda^*(B_{\text{crit}}^-)

Thus \lambda^* jumps upward at each activation threshold. \blacksquare

---

A.5. Proof of Lemma 3.10 (Friction-Adjusted Peak)

Statement. Under Axiom 2.1, E_i^\tau(A_i) = D_i(A_i)/(A_i + \tau_i) has a unique maximum A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger, characterized by:

D_i'(A_i^{\dagger,\tau}) = \frac{D_i(A_i^{\dagger,\tau})}{A_i^{\dagger,\tau} + \tau_i}

Proof. Differentiating:

\frac{dE_i^\tau}{dA_i} = \frac{(A_i + \tau_i) D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i)}{(A_i + \tau_i)^2}

Set g_i^\tau(A_i) := (A_i + \tau_i)D_i'(A_i) - D_i(A_i). Then:

g_i^{\tau\prime}(A_i) = (A_i + \tau_i) D_i''(A_i)

On (0, A_{0,i}]: D_i'' > 0, so g_i^{\tau\prime} > 0, hence g_i^\tau increasing. Since g_i^\tau(0) = \tau_i D_i'(0) > 0, g_i^\tau > 0, and E_i^\tau is increasing.

On (A_{0,i}, \infty): D_i'' < 0, so g_i^{\tau\prime} < 0, hence g_i^\tau decreasing. At A_i^\dagger:

g_i^\tau(A_i^\dagger) = (A_i^\dagger + \tau_i) D_i'(A_i^\dagger) - D_i(A_i^\dagger) = \tau_i D_i'(A_i^\dagger) > 0

As A_i \to \infty, g_i^\tau(A_i) \to -D_{\max,i} < 0. By IVT, there exists a unique root A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger. \blacksquare

---

A.6. Proof of Theorem 3.11 (Friction-Induced Threshold Inflation)

Statement. In the optimal solution to (\mathcal{P}_\tau), for every i:

A_i^* = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad A_i^* \geq A_i^{\dagger,\tau} > A_i^\dagger

Proof. Analogous to Theorem 3.4, replacing E_i with E_i^\tau and A_i^\dagger with A_i^{\dagger,\tau}. If A_i^* \in (0, A_i^{\dagger,\tau}), then E_i^\tau(A_i^*) < E_i^\tau(A_i^{\dagger,\tau}), and the same reallocation argument yields a strict improvement. \blacksquare

