Appendix D: Empirical Validation Protocol

D.1. Data Sources

Primary Dataset. SCOPE-Bench (Deng et al., 2026) provides CDS annotations for 150K short videos with approximately 1M user-item interactions from 10K users. CDS is a seven-level rubric grounded in cognitive psychology and learning theories, measuring the extent to which a video is expected to stimulate higher-order cognitive processes.

Supplementary Dataset. A paired corpus of N = 500 content items stratified by:

· CDS level (1–7)
· Visibility decile (1–10)
· Content type (educational, entertainment, news, lifestyle)

D.2. A_h Estimation Protocol

1. Behavioral subsample: n = 5000 users with full behavioral traces.
2. Physiological subsample: n = 50 users with pupillometry, eye-tracking, and EEG.
3. Calibration: Fit IRT model on physiological subsample; predict A_h for behavioral subsample.
4. Validation: Compare predicted A_h against self-reported attention and comprehension tests.

D.3. Falsification Tests

Test 1: Validity.

· Hypothesis: D \uparrow \Rightarrow measured learning \uparrow
· Operationalization: Pre/post comprehension tests on a subset of content.
· Null: No significant correlation between CDS and learning gains.

Test 2: Efficiency.

· Hypothesis: \Phi \uparrow \Rightarrow (cognitive outcome)/(attention expenditure) \uparrow
· Operationalization: Compute \Phi = D/A_h for each item; correlate with efficiency ratio.
· Null: No significant correlation.

Test 3: Decoupling (central).

· Hypothesis: V(C_1) > V(C_2) \not\Rightarrow \Phi(C_1) > \Phi(C_2)
· Operationalization: Spearman rank correlation \rho_s(V, \widehat{\Phi}).
· Null: \rho_s \geq 0
· Alternative: \rho_s < 0
· Rejection region: Reject null at \alpha = 0.05 if \rho_s < -0.1 with bootstrap confidence interval excluding 0.

D.4. Pre-Registration

The protocol should be registered on OSF or AsPredicted before data collection, specifying:

· Hypotheses and decision rules
· Sample size justification (power analysis for \rho_s)
· Exclusion criteria
· Analysis plan

