Appendix E: Extended Collision Analysis

E.1. Rational Inattention

Source. Sims (2003); Matějka & McKay (2015).

Formalization. The rational inattention problem is:

W(\mathcal{A}, \boldsymbol{\pi}, \lambda) = \max_{\boldsymbol{P} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{A})^I} \sum_{i=1}^I \sum_{\boldsymbol{a} \in \mathcal{A}} \pi_i P_i(\boldsymbol{a}) a_i - \lambda \mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{P}, \boldsymbol{\pi})

where \mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{P}, \boldsymbol{\pi}) is the mutual information between prior and posterior, and \lambda > 0 translates informational burden into utils.

Collision verdict: VERY STRONG. The shadow price \lambda in our model is the direct analogue of the rational inattention cost parameter. The budget-constrained allocation with shadow price is already formalized.

Our position. We inherit the rational inattention framework. Our contribution is not the shadow price concept but its application to cultural content allocation with non-convex production functions.

E.2. Information Foraging

Source. Pirolli & Card (1999).

Formalization. The prey choice model states:

R = \frac{G}{T_B + T_W} = \frac{G_f - C_f}{T_B + T_W}

where G is net gain, T_B is between-patch time, and T_W is within-patch time. The diet condition is r_i = u_i/t_i \geq R_{\text{media}}.

Collision verdict: VERY STRONG. The structure is exactly analogous to our allocation problem.

Our position. The critical distinction is T \neq A_h. Information foraging measures handling time t_i, not attentional expenditure A_h. Our framework replaces the temporal proxy with a latent attentional construct.

E.3. Cognitive Load Theory

Source. Paas & Van Merriënboer (1993, 1994).

Formalization. Instructional efficiency:

E = \frac{\text{Performance}}{\text{Mental Effort}}

Paas and Van Merriënboer (1994) demonstrated that students who studied worked examples invested less time and mental effort while attaining better and less effort-demanding transfer performance.

Collision verdict: VERY STRONG. The efficiency construct E is structurally identical to \Phi = D/A_h.

Our position. We transpose this construct outside the educational frame to algorithmic cultural ecosystems.

E.4. Cognitive Production Function

Source. Wharton (2026) working paper.

Formalization. The cognitive production function is:

Q(L) = \max \left(1 - \left(\frac{L}{C} + \Phi\right)^{1-\alpha}, 0\right)

where L is mental effort, C is complexity, and \alpha parametrizes concavity. The sparsity threshold for \alpha > 1 is:

\frac{p}{w C} < A(\alpha), \quad A(\alpha) = \frac{1}{\alpha - 1}

so that optimal attention is 0 iff this condition holds.

Collision verdict: VERY STRONG. The formal structure — cognitive production function, optimal allocation, sparsity threshold — is exactly the architecture we propose.

Our position. We apply this structure to cultural content under AI abundance.

E.5. Content Depth Score (CDS)

Source. Deng et al. (2026).

Formalization. CDS is a seven-level rubric grounded in cognitive psychology and learning theories. SCOPE-Bench provides CDS annotations for 150K videos and roughly 1M user-item interactions from 10K users. Evaluating 13 representative recommender systems reveals a consistent preference for shallow-content videos.

Collision verdict: VERY STRONG. D(C) is not our novelty.

Our position. CDS is a candidate instrument for D. The gap is the normalization by attention and the embedding within a non-convex allocation framework.

E.6. Platform Search Distortion

Source. Cai, Gautier, & Wolthoff (2026).

Formalization. A monopoly platform sets meeting rates between buyers and two seller types. The platform keeps high-quality niche attention just past the saturation point — where extra exposure stops creating rents and starts eroding them — and diverts the rest to mass-market sellers. Applying the model to Amazon and Google data indicates buyer-surplus losses of 63% and 44% of the planner's benchmark.

Collision verdict: STRONG.

Our position. The saturation point concept is directly analogous to our friction-adjusted threshold A_i^{\dagger,\tau}.

E.7. Sigmoidal Production Functions

Source. Knapsack Problems with Sigmoid Utilities (UCSB); Learning curve literature.

Formalization. The learning curve follows the Sigmoid function:

AK_t(a_{i,l}) = \frac{1}{1 + \left(\frac{1}{AK_{i_0}} - 1\right) e^{-\gamma_i T_p(ak_i^l)}}

where T_p represents continuous performance time and \gamma_i is learning ability. Sigmoid functions impart a combinatorial element to optimization variables and make global optimization computationally hard.

Collision verdict: VALIDATED. The sigmoidal form is consistent with existing frameworks.

Our position. The novel contribution is the derivation of the minimal activation threshold A_i^\dagger and the proof that no optimal allocation falls in the sub-threshold region.

