Appendix G: Extended Proofs for Platform Distortions

G.1. Proof of Proposition 4.4 (Interior Solution)

Statement. Under monotonicity and concavity, any interior solution satisfies:

D_i'(A_i^*) = \lambda

for all i with A_i^* > 0, where \lambda is the Lagrange multiplier associated with the budget constraint.

Proof. The Lagrangian is:

\mathcal{L} = \sum_i D_i(A_i) - \lambda\left(\sum_i A_i - B\right)

The first-order condition for interior A_i^* is D_i'(A_i^*) - \lambda = 0. The second-order condition is satisfied by concavity. \blacksquare

G.2. Proof of Corollary 4.5 (Shadow Price of Attention)

Statement. \lambda is the marginal cognitive value of the attentional budget. It is not equal to the average efficiency D/A in general.

Proof. By the envelope theorem:

\frac{\partial}{\partial B} \left( \max_{\{A_i\}} \sum_i D_i(A_i) \right) = \lambda

The shadow price \lambda is the derivative of the value function with respect to B. The average efficiency D/A is a different object — it measures the average return per unit of attention, not the marginal return. They coincide only if D_i is linear. \blacksquare

G.3. Proof of Corollary 4.9 (Aggregate vs. Marginal)

Statement. \Phi measures per-unit efficiency, not aggregate cognitive value.

Proof. By definition, \Phi = D/A. This is a ratio. The aggregate cognitive value is \sum_i D_i(A_i). A content item with high \Phi but low total D may contribute less to civilization than a content item with low \Phi but high total D. \blacksquare

